It took me two months to sign all of these.

42
00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:21.260
Yeah.

43
00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:22.440
This was a big project.

44
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:23.220
Yeah.

45
00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:25.180
But they're out there.

46
00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:26.260
They are available.

47
00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:30.140
You can find a link on our officeladies.com website.

48
00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:38.460
We're going to put it up on our socials, and you can also find it at hc.com slash office BFFs.

49
00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:40.860
We signed them with love, you guys.

50
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.400
We signed them with love.

51
00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:45.000
Okay, morning announcements are done.

52
00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:47.440
Let's get in to this episode.

53
00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:51.160
Today, we are breaking down Koi Pond.

54
00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:59.600
It is Season 6, Episode 8, written by Warren Lieberstein and Halstead Sullivan and directed by Reggie Hudlin.

55
00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:01.420
Here is your summary.

56
00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:03.380
It's very simple.

57
00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:04.800
It really is.

58
00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:07.520
But it packs a big splash.

59
00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:07.920
What?

60
00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:16.960
Michael falls into a koi pond while out for a meeting and becomes the butt of many office jokes.

61
00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:24.700
Meanwhile, Pam and Andy go out on mandated cold calls and are mistaken for a married couple all day.

62
00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:27.800
A married couple expecting a baby.

63
00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:28.840
Mm-hmm.

64
00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:30.600
A very funny runner.

65
00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:31.380
I enjoyed it.

66
00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:33.420
Fast fact number one.

67
00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:39.040
This episode was directed by legend Reggie Hudlin.

68
00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:51.740
You know, it happened from time to time on The Office when we would get these directors that you just can't believe that they are directing your television show.

69
00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:52.340
Yeah.

70
00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:54.160
And Reggie was one of them.

71
00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:57.780
So Reggie wrote and directed the film House Party.

72
00:03:58.040 --> 00:03:59.880
He directed the film Boomerang.

73
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.220
He directed 11 episodes of The Bernie Mac Show.

74
00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:07.460
He was the president of Black Entertainment Television for three years.

75
00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:15.320
And he went on to be a producer on Quentin Tarantino's Django Unchained, which was nominated for Best Picture at the Academy Awards.

76
00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:18.140
Reggie was amazing to work with.

77
00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:19.980
He was just great.

78
00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:29.060
And in addition to all of his professional accomplishments, his family history is amazing.

79
00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:35.200
I knew a little bit of it, but I didn't know everything in researching this episode.

80
00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:41.260
I went to his website, and his bio, his family bio, is worth a read.

81
00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:42.900
I will give you the highlights.

82
00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:46.540
His family grew up in East St. Louis.

83
00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:53.180
His great-great-grandparents were Peter and Nancy Hudlin, who were part of the Underground Railroad.

84
00:04:53.600 --> 00:05:00.480
His great-uncle was a very famous tennis instructor who mentored Arthur Ashe and Althea Gibson.

85
00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:05.540
Reggie attended Harvard University, where he graduated magna cum laude with a B.A.

86
00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:07.780
in Visual and Environmental Studies.

87
00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:15.140
And it was at Harvard that he directed his thesis project, which was a short film version of House Party.

88
00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:19.080
It was inspired by his experience growing up in East St. Louis.

89
00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:24.280
And that short film became the basis of his feature film, House Party.

90
00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:27.880
Well, we reached out to Warren and Halstead, the writers of this episode.

91
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:31.820
They are going to share awesome, awesome stuff with us we can't wait to share with you.

92
00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:39.080
Warren said that one of the perks of writing on The Office was being able to meet some of the people in the industry you really admired.

93
00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:45.420
He was a big fan of House Party and Boomerang, so he was thrilled Reggie was going to direct the episode.

94
00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:49.980
And he said Reggie was, quote, all class, and we had a great week together.

95
00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:52.140
It was an honor working with him.

96
00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:57.180
Yeah, and Halstead also mentioned working with Reggie when we reached out.

97
00:05:57.320 --> 00:06:03.320
And he just said that Reggie had an amazing sense of humor, and he knew how to land the jokes.

98
00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:07.080
He also said that Reggie was a great storyteller.

99
00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:13.680
He said he doesn't think he told stories with the intention of Halstead repeating any of them on a podcast,

100
00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:18.860
but he just wanted to say that he was really great to work with, and he'd really like to work with him again.

101
00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:20.920
I feel the same way.

102
00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:23.660
I mean, I just have wonderful memories of doing this episode.

103
00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:24.260
Same.

104
00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:27.380
All right, now for fast fact number two, Ange.

105
00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:33.400
Warren shared with us that there was a very, very unique writing style for this episode,

106
00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:35.460
and I found it totally fascinating.

107
00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:42.400
He said that with most episodes of The Office, and including most shows he's ever worked on,

108
00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:46.640
they lay out the story on index cards, which we've talked about.

109
00:06:46.940 --> 00:06:50.220
You love the index cards on the wall of the writer's room.

110
00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:52.800
I love an organizational technique.

111
00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:59.340
But he said that for the most part, the only thing you write down on those cards are just the main beats of the story.

112
00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:03.540
And you can move them around, you know, and then you figure out the flow,

113
00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:09.140
and then you make an outline, and you use that outline to make your first draft of the script.

114
00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:16.560
But with Koi Pond, Warren shared that so much of what they were telling was a subtextual story

115
00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:23.560
between Michael and Jim, that Greg had them do something different when they were writing this episode.

116
00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:29.660
He said that they had to write the main story beats on white note cards,

117
00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:35.120
and then they wrote all of the subtext on pink note cards.

118
00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:40.440
So anything that Michael and Jim were feeling went on pink, and like plot went on white.

119
00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:45.160
And he said it was really helpful for this episode, and he gave some examples.

120
00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:51.200
So he said early on, Michael has a talking head where he wants to insult Jim.

121
00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:56.360
And he starts off by saying that if Jim wants to be the boss, then he'll have to wear Michael's suit,

122
00:07:56.460 --> 00:07:58.060
which would be too small for him.

123
00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:05.400
But then Michael stops, and he admits that, you know, Jim would probably still look good in it.

124
00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:06.260
Yeah, it's so great.

125
00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:08.040
He's like, damn it, he'd still look good.

126
00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:14.440
So the text is that he's mad at Jim, but the subtext is that he's still envious of him.

127
00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:16.980
So envy went on the pink card.

128
00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:23.380
And then, of course, at the very end of the episode, Michael gets to have this talking head

129
00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:27.480
where he realizes that, wait, Jim is actually jealous of him.

130
00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:33.220
And so I just thought that was really, really interesting, the idea of mapping out the subtext.

131
00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:34.460
I love that.

132
00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:37.880
I mean, that's something we would do in our scripts as actors, like our actory homework.

133
00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:44.020
We would get a script with like a paragraph that's like, Angela is mean to Oscar, right?

134
00:08:44.020 --> 00:08:50.140
And there's a speech where she's mean to Oscar, but the subtext is Philip was turned away from

135
00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:51.220
daycare, right?

136
00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:54.920
So she's coming in with like shame and embarrassment.

137
00:08:55.420 --> 00:09:03.880
So in her angry speech to Oscar about him using her stapler, the subtext is Angela's embarrassed,

138
00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:04.540
right?

139
00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:04.840
Yeah.

140
00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:06.580
So we would do that kind of thing.

141
00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:08.820
But I love that they did that in the writer's room, too.

142
00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:11.240
It's like a really cool creative writing exercise.

143
00:09:11.860 --> 00:09:12.340
Yeah.

144
00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:16.880
Well, Angela, this brings us to fast fact number three.

145
00:09:17.180 --> 00:09:20.180
Oh, I'm so excited for fast fact number three.

146
00:09:20.420 --> 00:09:23.800
This is not Angela's digital clutter this week, folks.

147
00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:25.500
Sorry to disappoint.

148
00:09:26.060 --> 00:09:34.460
Guys, the plot of Michael falling into a koi pond while on his way to a meeting is based

149
00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:35.780
on a real life event.

150
00:09:35.780 --> 00:09:40.380
This happened to Warren, you guys, the writer of this episode.

151
00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:49.100
Warren and Halstead together went on a meeting and Warren fell into a pond in a lobby.

152
00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:55.080
And here they are today to share the whole story with us.

153
00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:59.780
Yes, they each sent in audio clips and they were so fun.

154
00:10:00.100 --> 00:10:05.940
We decided to turn them into a fully sound produced story thanks to our own Sam Kiefer.

155
00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:12.640
So here it is, the inspiration of koi pond by Warren and Halstead.

156
00:10:12.640 --> 00:10:21.530
Hi, my name is Warren Lieberstein.

157
00:10:21.730 --> 00:10:26.290
And along with Halstead Sullivan, we wrote the koi pond episode of The Office.

158
00:10:26.290 --> 00:10:31.760
The idea for the story stemmed from my falling into a koi pond.

159
00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:36.860
What had happened was our agents set up a meeting for Halstead and I on the west side

160
00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:37.680
of Los Angeles.

161
00:10:38.060 --> 00:10:41.160
It was in this nice two-story office building.

162
00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:45.400
There was a marble reception, an open atrium lobby.

163
00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:47.320
There were these dark taverne floors.

164
00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:53.260
So we enter into this building for the meeting and I'm chewing gum.

165
00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:55.360
And I can't go to a meeting chewing gum.

166
00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:57.060
That's horribly unprofessional.

167
00:10:57.060 --> 00:10:59.480
And so I'm immediately looking for a trash can.

168
00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:05.500
Warren and I walk in for this meeting and, you know, I head over to the receptionist

169
00:11:05.500 --> 00:11:06.480
just to announce our names.

170
00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:09.140
And Warren says, oh, I'm going to go throw away my gum.

171
00:11:09.880 --> 00:11:12.200
I see a trash can by the staircase off the corner.

172
00:11:12.340 --> 00:11:13.200
So I walk over.

173
00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:18.360
After I toss my gum in the trash, I turn and pivot on my right foot.

174
00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:22.200
My right foot somehow, though, doesn't reach the floor.

175
00:11:23.260 --> 00:11:26.360
So while I'm there at the desk, you know, checking in,

176
00:11:26.360 --> 00:11:29.260
suddenly I hear this huge splash.

177
00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:36.900
So my right foot doesn't find the floor and I lose my balance and I'm falling backwards.

178
00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:41.860
At this point, real concerns that I'm falling to my death.

179
00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:44.100
Down an open stairwell or whatever.

180
00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:51.620
My legs hit it first and I have no idea what size tank of water I'm falling into.

181
00:11:52.180 --> 00:11:59.500
In this millisecond, I now believe I'm falling into a shark tank and I'm definitely going to die by shark attack.

182
00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:04.320
But after a second or so, I realize, obviously, I'm in a koi pond.

183
00:12:04.620 --> 00:12:06.640
I just nesty plunged into it.

184
00:12:06.760 --> 00:12:07.940
I am drenched.

185
00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:13.060
And all I can say over and over again is, oh, my God.

186
00:12:16.980 --> 00:12:19.040
Halstead turns around and sees me.

187
00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:24.380
Warren is basically like lying in the pond splashing going, oh, my God.

188
00:12:24.500 --> 00:12:25.020
Oh, my God.

189
00:12:25.140 --> 00:12:25.580
Oh, my God.

190
00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:27.140
I'm like, Warren, Warren, Warren.

191
00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:31.000
As Warren's sitting there splashing in the pond, we look up.

192
00:12:31.220 --> 00:12:34.540
Of course, everyone comes out of their offices to see what the commotion is.

193
00:12:34.620 --> 00:12:37.380
So basically everyone in this building is now staring.

194
00:12:37.380 --> 00:12:41.880
And on the railing, there are like eight people looking over, you know, on the first floor.

195
00:12:42.020 --> 00:12:44.200
All these people have come out to see all the commotion.

196
00:12:45.100 --> 00:12:51.480
My favorite part of Warren's description is the nesty plunge into the koi pond.

197
00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:54.440
And also just like how disorienting that is.

198
00:12:54.580 --> 00:12:54.880
Yeah.

199
00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:56.640
Like it's going to take you a minute.

200
00:12:56.840 --> 00:12:57.740
It's so many things.

201
00:12:57.900 --> 00:12:58.740
It's like I'm falling.

202
00:12:58.760 --> 00:12:59.620
I'm wet.

203
00:12:59.840 --> 00:13:00.800
Why am I wet?

204
00:13:00.940 --> 00:13:01.860
Where am I?

205
00:13:02.860 --> 00:13:07.160
So I'm drenched and there's so much water on this nice lobby floor.

206
00:13:07.380 --> 00:13:13.180
And someone comes up to us with a roll of those brown paper towels that you get from

207
00:13:13.180 --> 00:13:19.960
the bathroom, you know, that are just basically made of manila paper and have zero absorbency.

208
00:13:19.960 --> 00:13:21.440
And it's like, OK, thanks.

209
00:13:22.860 --> 00:13:25.660
At this point, they leave, right?

210
00:13:25.840 --> 00:13:27.300
Because Warren is soaking wet.

211
00:13:27.580 --> 00:13:29.020
That's what you would think, Jenna.

212
00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:34.440
But you have to remember, we all live in Los Angeles and they had driven really far for

213
00:13:34.440 --> 00:13:34.940
this meeting.

214
00:13:36.080 --> 00:13:41.080
They actually offer us to move the meeting for a week later.

215
00:13:41.380 --> 00:13:46.760
But Halstead wanted to keep the meeting because, you know, for those who don't live in L.A.,

216
00:13:46.760 --> 00:13:50.980
going to the west side, no one wants to cross the 405 ever.

217
00:13:51.360 --> 00:13:52.440
The 405 is a highway.

218
00:13:52.620 --> 00:13:59.060
And no matter what side of it you're on, traveling to the other is like a traffic nightmare.

219
00:13:59.060 --> 00:14:03.180
We know that around the corner is a sporting goods store.

220
00:14:03.340 --> 00:14:05.020
So we go there.

221
00:14:05.320 --> 00:14:06.460
You know, we walk in.

222
00:14:06.900 --> 00:14:07.940
Warren's soaking wet.

223
00:14:08.880 --> 00:14:09.960
I'm sopping wet.

224
00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:11.260
There was these two women working there.

225
00:14:11.320 --> 00:14:14.660
And I looked insane, like Jim Carrey in Ace Ventura when he comes out of the bathroom.

226
00:14:14.960 --> 00:14:16.320
Water's pouring all over their floor.

227
00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:21.140
And I just say, I need one of everything.

228
00:14:21.140 --> 00:14:25.340
We go back to the meeting and Halstead looks, you know, great, per usual.

229
00:14:25.580 --> 00:14:27.140
You know, it's his normal meeting outfit.

230
00:14:27.900 --> 00:14:29.820
And I look like a rap star from the 80s.

231
00:14:29.900 --> 00:14:31.340
It was a sports clothing store.

232
00:14:31.480 --> 00:14:33.900
So, you know, all I could get really was a tracksuit.

233
00:14:34.260 --> 00:14:35.260
But we take the meeting.

234
00:14:35.260 --> 00:14:39.400
And of course, all we can really talk about is me falling into the koi pun.

235
00:14:39.580 --> 00:14:41.640
And the puns were flying.

