00:39:12.590 --> 00:39:17.170
And, like, a couple hours later, she got back to me and was like,

742
00:39:17.330 --> 00:39:18.830
so I couldn't find any pictures.

743
00:39:19.090 --> 00:39:22.830
I called the Steamtown Mall, got a hold of someone.

744
00:39:22.830 --> 00:39:27.370
They were very weirded out by her asking if there's a fountain there.

745
00:39:27.490 --> 00:39:30.830
Why are all these people calling and asking if there's a fountain?

746
00:39:32.490 --> 00:39:32.970
Yeah.

747
00:39:33.550 --> 00:39:36.270
So they, like, were like, well, we'll get your name and phone number,

748
00:39:36.270 --> 00:39:37.950
and someone will contact you.

749
00:39:38.230 --> 00:39:42.210
And then an hour later, she just got a text message from them saying, no, we don't.

750
00:39:42.510 --> 00:39:43.210
All right.

751
00:39:43.870 --> 00:39:45.870
I love this story so much.

752
00:39:46.030 --> 00:39:50.370
I love that both of you women were calling the Steamtown Mall,

753
00:39:50.370 --> 00:39:53.050
asking if there's a fountain, and the people are like,

754
00:39:53.150 --> 00:39:55.530
what do these ladies want to do in a fountain?

755
00:39:55.770 --> 00:39:57.890
Why are they looking for a fountain so badly?

756
00:39:58.250 --> 00:40:01.890
I want to picture the person answering the phone, like, later that day.

757
00:40:02.030 --> 00:40:04.430
Maybe they're on their break, you know, having a snack.

758
00:40:04.450 --> 00:40:06.290
They're with other co-workers, and they're like,

759
00:40:06.830 --> 00:40:10.330
you guys getting calls, like, with people asking if we have a fountain?

760
00:40:10.610 --> 00:40:11.530
Like, what the hell?

761
00:40:12.890 --> 00:40:14.190
Thank you, Cassie.

762
00:40:14.350 --> 00:40:16.350
But, Victoria, you are right.

763
00:40:16.350 --> 00:40:18.730
There is no fountain in the Steamtown Mall.

764
00:40:19.010 --> 00:40:19.490
Yes.

765
00:40:20.410 --> 00:40:20.930
Okay.

766
00:40:21.810 --> 00:40:25.710
Well, Angela, I have a mystery I want to solve from this scene,

767
00:40:26.350 --> 00:40:29.510
and I'm calling it Ryan has glasses.

768
00:40:30.410 --> 00:40:34.630
Remember last episode, he was wearing his Trilby hat?

769
00:40:34.930 --> 00:40:35.050
Yes.

770
00:40:35.990 --> 00:40:38.550
Well, now he's holding a pair of glasses.

771
00:40:38.710 --> 00:40:40.070
They never go on his face.

772
00:40:40.330 --> 00:40:43.010
After I saw this, I watched the whole episode,

773
00:40:43.230 --> 00:40:45.550
waiting for those glasses to go on his face.

774
00:40:45.710 --> 00:40:47.650
But he's just gesturing with them.

775
00:40:47.930 --> 00:40:48.550
What's up?

776
00:40:48.550 --> 00:40:50.050
He's got a pair of fake glasses.

777
00:40:50.290 --> 00:40:51.610
I don't know what he's going through.

778
00:40:51.930 --> 00:40:53.590
Ryan and the accessories.

779
00:40:53.990 --> 00:40:54.190
Yeah.

780
00:40:54.190 --> 00:40:55.950
He's wanting to feel dignified or something.

781
00:40:56.210 --> 00:40:57.250
I guess so.

782
00:40:57.530 --> 00:41:00.750
Michael wants other people to shout out things they don't want to be teased about.

783
00:41:01.090 --> 00:41:02.390
And Dwight volunteers.

784
00:41:02.390 --> 00:41:05.230
He says, my nose is too small.

785
00:41:05.410 --> 00:41:07.450
And then he has a talking head where he says,

786
00:41:07.910 --> 00:41:11.310
the geometric proportions of my face are perfect in every way but one.

787
00:41:11.550 --> 00:41:13.030
My nose is too small.

788
00:41:13.390 --> 00:41:14.410
I mean, it works.

789
00:41:14.410 --> 00:41:15.810
I can still smell things.

790
00:41:15.930 --> 00:41:18.770
I just have to be a lot closer than most people.

791
00:41:19.010 --> 00:41:22.430
And then Rain, as Dwight, does this little, like, sniffy sniff.

792
00:41:22.670 --> 00:41:23.750
It's really cute, actually.

793
00:41:23.750 --> 00:41:24.530
I thought it was cute.

794
00:41:24.530 --> 00:41:27.510
We got so much mail about this, Angela.

795
00:41:27.950 --> 00:41:38.750
People really wanted to know if Rain participated in this, the writing of this, or if it was a total surprise to him when he got the script.

796
00:41:38.750 --> 00:41:40.790
Well, here's what Halstead said.

797
00:41:41.030 --> 00:41:48.650
He said they asked Rain if there was anything about him that people made fun of or that he was ever sensitive about growing up.

798
00:41:48.830 --> 00:41:52.090
And Rain said, okay, my nose being small.

799
00:41:52.690 --> 00:41:55.110
And it actually worked as a really funny bit.

800
00:41:55.290 --> 00:41:58.010
Obviously, Rain is fine with his nose now, you know.

801
00:41:58.390 --> 00:41:58.650
Yes.

802
00:41:58.810 --> 00:42:03.550
But Halstead said they liked to reach out to the actors to get things.

803
00:42:03.550 --> 00:42:09.770
That way, it's better coming from the actor than the writer making something up that maybe, I guess, could hurt your feelings, right?

804
00:42:10.070 --> 00:42:10.430
Yeah.

805
00:42:10.590 --> 00:42:12.330
No, they were always really good about that.

806
00:42:12.470 --> 00:42:13.310
That's very funny.

807
00:42:13.590 --> 00:42:15.910
As this scene goes on, other people participate.

808
00:42:15.910 --> 00:42:22.850
And I zoomed in on the dry erase board at the end to see what Michael had written on the board under each person's name.

809
00:42:22.850 --> 00:42:37.850
The Do Not Mock list has Michael, Koi Pond and Fountain, Dwight, Nose Too Small, Aaron, Orphan, Kevin, Huge Gut, Angela, Shrimpy, Meredith, Sex with a TerroriSt.

810
00:42:38.550 --> 00:42:39.230
Shrimpy?

811
00:42:39.990 --> 00:42:40.670
Shrimpy?

812
00:42:40.810 --> 00:42:42.250
So I was like, what?

813
00:42:42.510 --> 00:42:44.970
So I went to the script.

814
00:42:45.750 --> 00:42:46.890
Shrimpy is not in the script.

815
00:42:47.250 --> 00:42:50.930
It says, for Angela, Michael writes, tiny bitch.

816
00:42:52.050 --> 00:42:56.330
And I think Steve, in the moment, just wrote Shrimpy.

817
00:42:56.950 --> 00:43:02.430
Well, there is a deleted scene for Aaron explaining why they wrote Orphan.

818
00:43:02.650 --> 00:43:03.170
That's right.

819
00:43:03.670 --> 00:43:06.450
Leaving the conference room, we are back with Pam and Andy.

820
00:43:06.590 --> 00:43:08.710
They are at their next sales call.

821
00:43:08.850 --> 00:43:13.310
They are with Kina Gifford, played by Amy Reid.

822
00:43:13.850 --> 00:43:16.650
Well, Angela, we know Amy Reid very well.

823
00:43:16.650 --> 00:43:19.990
I thought perhaps you'd like to give us the guest star breakdown on this one.

824
00:43:19.990 --> 00:43:21.650
Oh, I would love to.

825
00:43:22.030 --> 00:43:26.030
So Amy Reid, we knew her at this time as Amy Weaver.

826
00:43:26.930 --> 00:43:30.030
And she is just a dear friend of mine.

827
00:43:30.130 --> 00:43:32.170
And I've known her a long time.

828
00:43:32.390 --> 00:43:33.830
We met working at IOS.

829
00:43:33.830 --> 00:43:38.310
I think our bonding moment was the song Jolene came on.

830
00:43:38.490 --> 00:43:39.850
And she's from Oklahoma.

831
00:43:39.890 --> 00:43:42.490
And my family's from Texas and Louisiana.

832
00:43:42.490 --> 00:43:52.440
And we both, without missing a beat, just started going, Jolene, Jolene, Jolene, Jolene, in our southern accents.

833
00:43:52.980 --> 00:43:54.660
I just love her so much.

834
00:43:54.740 --> 00:43:57.740
She is a hilarious actress and phenomenal writer.

835
00:43:58.160 --> 00:44:00.840
She's still one of my dearest friends to this day.

836
00:44:00.840 --> 00:44:07.260
And I reached out to Amy and asked her if she could share her memories of being on the set of The Office.

837
00:44:07.720 --> 00:44:09.440
And here's what she had to share.

838
00:44:10.220 --> 00:44:16.020
First of all, she said her scenes were only with Jenna and Ed in this super small abandoned office building.

839
00:44:16.300 --> 00:44:20.480
There wasn't a huge cast or crew, so she really got to hang out, which is fun.

840
00:44:20.480 --> 00:44:26.380
She said one of the things she remembers is that Jenna was wearing a really huge baby bump.

841
00:44:27.140 --> 00:44:29.700
And it was making her go to the bathroom.

842
00:44:30.680 --> 00:44:35.060
And she went to the bathroom one time right before they called them back to set.

843
00:44:35.320 --> 00:44:42.240
And some very sweet but maybe a little too eager production assistant was knocking nonstop on the door

844
00:44:42.240 --> 00:44:45.660
while poor Jenna was trying to get the belly back on.

845
00:44:45.660 --> 00:44:54.600
Then, Amy said, I only really appreciated how hot and heavy those suckers are many years later on a shoot where I had to be fake pregnant.

846
00:44:55.000 --> 00:45:00.540
She said she would like everyone to know even fake pregnant women should not be rushed in the bathroom.

847
00:45:00.760 --> 00:45:01.940
That's the lesson here.

848
00:45:03.660 --> 00:45:05.040
I knew you'd be happy.

849
00:45:05.220 --> 00:45:06.040
I know you're applauding.

850
00:45:06.280 --> 00:45:08.540
I am slow clapping that.

851
00:45:08.780 --> 00:45:10.480
Amy, thank you.

852
00:45:10.700 --> 00:45:12.180
I feel so validated.

853
00:45:13.520 --> 00:45:16.380
Let me explain what you have on.

854
00:45:16.460 --> 00:45:18.040
It's not just a belly.

855
00:45:18.300 --> 00:45:23.660
So I was wearing, for a reason I don't know, tights.

856
00:45:23.860 --> 00:45:24.200
Yeah.

857
00:45:24.680 --> 00:45:26.980
So I've got underwear, tights.

858
00:45:27.160 --> 00:45:33.780
Then on top of that, you have on like what's like almost a wetsuit with boy shorts.

859
00:45:35.340 --> 00:45:38.760
And that goes all the way up over your bra.

860
00:45:39.040 --> 00:45:40.920
It's like a whole contraption.

861
00:45:40.920 --> 00:45:47.180
And then from there, they can slide in different sized bellies.

862
00:45:47.720 --> 00:45:50.480
Then I had all my clothes on top of that.

863
00:45:50.620 --> 00:45:55.140
But I also had on a microphone and a microphone pack.

864
00:45:55.360 --> 00:45:57.620
With a wire taped to you.

865
00:45:57.860 --> 00:45:58.360
Yes.

866
00:45:58.620 --> 00:46:03.440
So to go to the bathroom, I had to dismantle all of that.

867
00:46:03.640 --> 00:46:07.160
It was a good 10 minutes just to get undressed.

868
00:46:07.700 --> 00:46:09.280
Then I had to pee.

869
00:46:09.880 --> 00:46:11.860
Then I had to put it all on.

870
00:46:12.380 --> 00:46:12.900
Again.

871
00:46:13.280 --> 00:46:14.760
It was like 20 minutes.

872
00:46:15.140 --> 00:46:18.900
If I said I need to go to the bathroom, that was a 20 minute break.

873
00:46:18.900 --> 00:46:23.840
Because it was really difficult and frustrating.

874
00:46:25.060 --> 00:46:27.460
And no one seemed to get it.

875
00:46:27.980 --> 00:46:30.600
And I really like I asked Alicia.

876
00:46:30.820 --> 00:46:37.420
I was like, Alicia, is it worth like gathering the production team together and sort of like

877
00:46:37.420 --> 00:46:39.520
taking them through all the layers?

878
00:46:39.660 --> 00:46:44.840
Because they expect me to pee in the same amount of time that I always peed.

879
00:46:44.840 --> 00:46:47.600
But I'm wearing so many more elements.

880
00:46:48.540 --> 00:46:51.700
And maybe there's like a workshop we can do.

881
00:46:51.900 --> 00:46:52.780
To show them.

882
00:46:53.440 --> 00:46:53.840
Yes.

883
00:46:54.100 --> 00:46:57.920
And I actually think, Angela, correct me if I'm wrong.

884
00:46:58.920 --> 00:47:04.160
Wasn't there a day where Kelly Cantley, our first AD, put it on and wore it?

885
00:47:04.700 --> 00:47:05.100
Yes.

886
00:47:05.220 --> 00:47:08.040
Because you were like, someone else needs to experience this.

887
00:47:08.280 --> 00:47:08.800
Please.

888
00:47:08.980 --> 00:47:09.800
I'm begging you.

889
00:47:09.800 --> 00:47:14.640
And so she put it on, you know, years later, I would have to wear the same thing.

890
00:47:14.640 --> 00:47:18.660
And I feel like you paved the way with our crew about what to expect.

891
00:47:18.980 --> 00:47:19.720
I know.

892
00:47:19.860 --> 00:47:20.720
But you know what?

893
00:47:20.880 --> 00:47:22.080
It was a tough road.

894
00:47:22.260 --> 00:47:23.480
It was a tough paving.

895
00:47:23.780 --> 00:47:28.000
Well, after all these years, that's the first thing Amy remembered to share with me.

896
00:47:28.320 --> 00:47:29.320
Amy, thank you.

897
00:47:29.580 --> 00:47:30.280
Thank you.

898
00:47:30.440 --> 00:47:31.420
I'm so touched.

899
00:47:31.680 --> 00:47:35.140
She went on to say, Jenna and Ed and I were jammed into a tiny office.

900
00:47:35.400 --> 00:47:39.480
And the director, Reggie, and the crew were outside the door on the other side.

901
00:47:39.800 --> 00:47:46.140
And at some point during downtime between takes, Jenna and I got really chatty with each other.

902
00:47:46.240 --> 00:47:51.200
And I guess we were kind of leaving Ed out of the conversation with very little room to jump in.

903
00:47:51.240 --> 00:47:55.500
And he finally just interrupted and said, do you two know each other?

904
00:47:57.720 --> 00:48:02.200
Because you had met Amy through me and we had become all a group of friends.

905
00:48:02.600 --> 00:48:02.820
Yeah.

906
00:48:03.200 --> 00:48:03.560
Yeah.

907
00:48:03.560 --> 00:48:07.080
The last thing Amy shared was this.

908
00:48:07.680 --> 00:48:13.620
One random thing that made a huge impression on me that day was when Jenna invited me to her trailer for lunch.

909
00:48:13.780 --> 00:48:18.920
As we sat there chatting, it somehow came up about the beginning of you and her's friendship.

910
00:48:19.680 --> 00:48:24.120
Jenna shared about the transition you guys had by being on the cast of The Office,

911
00:48:24.120 --> 00:48:31.180
how you two really bonded over going from inconsistent acting careers to suddenly being in the limelight.

912
00:48:31.320 --> 00:48:35.360
And the impact and weight of being on The Office and the adjustment to that.

913
00:48:35.480 --> 00:48:38.720
And how you two were together through all of it.

914
00:48:38.880 --> 00:48:44.480
Through that transition, you balanced earnest humility and the thrill of being on the number one comedy.

915
00:48:45.320 --> 00:48:45.900
Wow.

916
00:48:46.140 --> 00:48:47.240
Now I'm like crying.

917
00:48:48.860 --> 00:48:50.520
I thought that was wonderful.

918
00:48:50.520 --> 00:48:51.260
Now I'm like really moved.

919
00:48:51.520 --> 00:48:52.160
I know.

920
00:48:52.820 --> 00:48:53.220
Yeah.

921
00:48:53.220 --> 00:48:55.580
I mean, that was true.

922
00:48:55.580 --> 00:49:00.400
I can't imagine having to have done that without you, Angela.

923
00:49:01.020 --> 00:49:01.980
Aw, lady.

924
00:49:02.480 --> 00:49:03.760
It was overwhelming.

925
00:49:03.760 --> 00:49:07.820
It was all of your professional dreams coming true,

926
00:49:07.820 --> 00:49:13.420
but all the ways that you don't think that that is going to impact your life in weird ways.

927
00:49:13.420 --> 00:49:20.320
You know, your face on television every week and what that does,

928
00:49:20.480 --> 00:49:23.520
how it changes different relationships or friendships,

929
00:49:24.080 --> 00:49:28.820
how your time is not your own when you are on a television show,

930
00:49:28.980 --> 00:49:36.000
the long hours, but then all of the additional promotional work that takes up your weekends

931
00:49:36.000 --> 00:49:40.480
and how you can't go to things anymore.

932
00:49:40.480 --> 00:49:44.540
And to have someone to go through that with and navigate that with.

933
00:49:44.620 --> 00:49:45.760
And you're so excited.

934
00:49:46.200 --> 00:49:51.080
But then the Midwesterner in me would always feel like very shy about it.

935
00:49:51.120 --> 00:49:52.780
And I think we had that in common.

936
00:49:54.040 --> 00:49:55.840
I mean, she really nailed it.

937
00:49:56.380 --> 00:49:56.740
I know.

938
00:49:56.880 --> 00:49:59.660
I thought, what great observations from her day on set.

939
00:50:00.340 --> 00:50:00.780
Yeah.

940
00:50:01.180 --> 00:50:03.140
Well, you know I feel the same way, lady.

941
00:50:03.340 --> 00:50:03.760
My gosh.

942
00:50:03.880 --> 00:50:09.660
How would I have gotten through, you know, the last 18 years, oh my word, without your friendship?

943
00:50:09.660 --> 00:50:11.080
Oh my gosh.

944
00:50:11.280 --> 00:50:11.780
18.

945
00:50:12.520 --> 00:50:15.480
I have one last fun tidbit about this scene.

946
00:50:16.180 --> 00:50:20.060
Warren reminded me of it, and I went and looked for it immediately.

947
00:50:20.760 --> 00:50:25.500
There is a photo of our daughter, Isabel, in a frame just over Amy's shoulder.

948
00:50:25.760 --> 00:50:31.000
If you go to 11 minutes, 35 seconds, you can see Isabel in the little silver frame.

949
00:50:31.180 --> 00:50:32.440
She's smiling so big.

950
00:50:32.440 --> 00:50:33.720
She's in her car seat.

951
00:50:33.720 --> 00:50:36.500
I love that Phil Shea did that for us.

952
00:50:36.780 --> 00:50:40.180
It just, oh my gosh, warmed my heart so much.

953
00:50:40.340 --> 00:50:41.420
I saw it too.

954
00:50:41.980 --> 00:50:44.280
Oh, oh, and one last thing.

955
00:50:44.420 --> 00:50:46.180
I know I've had a lot to say about this scene.

956
00:50:46.720 --> 00:50:52.740
So Miss Gifford and Andy bond over this birthing teacher named Miss Janet.

957
00:50:53.220 --> 00:50:55.200
Well, Janet is my sister's name.

958
00:50:56.080 --> 00:50:56.560
Hmm.

959
00:50:57.620 --> 00:50:58.800
That's so sweet.

960
00:50:59.060 --> 00:50:59.860
So there you go.

961
00:50:59.860 --> 00:51:02.280
That's everything that I had for that scene.

962
00:51:02.600 --> 00:51:04.360
Well, I think we should take a break.

963
00:51:04.400 --> 00:51:08.640
And when we come back, we're going to talk about how you eat like a squirrel, Angela.

964
00:51:09.140 --> 00:51:10.140
Okay, great.

965
00:51:19.100 --> 00:51:22.400
We are back, and we are still in the conference room.

966
00:51:22.960 --> 00:51:26.800
People are still talking about the things that bother them.

967
00:51:26.940 --> 00:51:30.300
And Angela, you do not like it when people say you eat like a squirrel.

968
00:51:30.740 --> 00:51:31.220
Mm-hmm.

969
00:51:31.760 --> 00:51:33.200
You don't care for that.

970
00:51:33.400 --> 00:51:34.440
I don't care for that.

971
00:51:34.440 --> 00:51:38.580
And also, I'm afraid someone's going to throw me around like a football because I'm so petite.

972
00:51:39.140 --> 00:51:39.480
Mm-hmm.

973
00:51:40.640 --> 00:51:49.700
Aaron is going to interrupt this meeting and let Michael know that someone has called to inform them that one of the coy died.

974
00:51:50.300 --> 00:51:52.040
And they want him to pay for it.

975
00:51:52.100 --> 00:51:53.320
It's going to be $300.

976
00:51:54.220 --> 00:51:56.200
Aren't coy, like, crazy expensive?

977
00:51:56.200 --> 00:51:58.200
Yeah, they are.

978
00:51:58.560 --> 00:52:11.520
And Halstead shared that they had pitched around in the writer's room a kind of extension of this storyline because they found out that some coy can, like, cost as much as $2,000.

979
00:52:11.520 --> 00:52:21.540
So, at one point, the coy that Michael killed was going to be worth $2,000, and that Michael was going to pay for it.

980
00:52:21.680 --> 00:52:26.380
He was going to get it because he was going to want to see a $2,000 fish.

981
00:52:26.640 --> 00:52:33.740
And then back at the office, he was going to cook it in the toaster oven and eat it.

982
00:52:33.860 --> 00:52:34.260
No!

983
00:52:36.140 --> 00:52:42.460
Yes, this was the pitch, but that ultimately Michael was going to chicken out and he was just going to throw it away.

984
00:52:43.260 --> 00:52:48.380
And Halstead said, by the time they got to the end of this pitch, they were like, you know what?

985
00:52:48.460 --> 00:52:49.320
It's disturbing.

986
00:52:49.560 --> 00:52:55.140
We're going to, how about the coy is just $300 and there's no eating of the coy.

987
00:52:55.440 --> 00:52:57.180
Or throwing it away.

988
00:52:57.560 --> 00:53:01.200
Yes, and Halstead said, I'm very happy we changed that.

989
00:53:01.680 --> 00:53:04.100
Well, you know, Michael wouldn't have thrown it away.

990
00:53:04.120 --> 00:53:07.280
If he didn't eat it, he would have had a proper burial for it.

991
00:53:07.500 --> 00:53:09.560
That's most likely, yes.

992
00:53:09.820 --> 00:53:11.420
And there would have been a coy funeral.

993
00:53:11.660 --> 00:53:14.600
Yes, and then maybe Creed would have digged it up and eaten it.

994
00:53:14.720 --> 00:53:15.660
Oh, gross.

995
00:53:17.080 --> 00:53:20.420
I did love when Creed was like, oh, man, you're paying way too much for worMs.

996
00:53:20.420 --> 00:53:21.320
Who's your worm guy?

997
00:53:22.080 --> 00:53:23.280
Who's your worm guy?

998
00:53:23.980 --> 00:53:24.760
So funny.

999
00:53:24.940 --> 00:53:25.960
Put that on a shirt.

1000
00:53:29.440 --> 00:53:33.740
Jim is going to get Michael aside and say, I have a strategy.

1001
00:53:34.340 --> 00:53:40.120
I think maybe you should make fun of yourself for falling into the coy pond.

1002
00:53:40.660 --> 00:53:43.260
Yeah, and that will diffuse everyone else, right?

1003
00:53:43.720 --> 00:53:44.140
Yeah.

1004
00:53:44.380 --> 00:53:46.620
They won't be able to tell jokes because you've covered it.

1005
00:53:47.100 --> 00:53:48.760
So Michael's going to try it.

1006
00:53:48.960 --> 00:53:50.660
He's like, all right, I'll try it.

1007
00:53:50.780 --> 00:53:53.920
And at first, it goes well.

1008
00:53:54.740 --> 00:53:55.780
And then it doesn't.

1009
00:53:55.780 --> 00:53:57.560
But he goes too far.

1010
00:53:57.660 --> 00:53:59.240
He goes too far.

1011
00:53:59.240 --> 00:54:01.200
And he knows he's going too far.

1012
00:54:01.400 --> 00:54:03.660
This was such a brilliant performance.

1013
00:54:04.020 --> 00:54:11.220
It's like that thing when you're like talking to someone and you know that moment you went past where you should have stopped.

1014
00:54:11.440 --> 00:54:13.740
And in your own brain, you're like, stop, stop.

1015
00:54:13.900 --> 00:54:14.660
But you can't.

1016
00:54:15.040 --> 00:54:15.420
Yes.

1017
00:54:15.560 --> 00:54:16.880
And he just keeps talking.

1018
00:54:17.200 --> 00:54:22.060
He keeps saying embarrassing things about his life that aren't even about the coy pond.

1019
00:54:22.540 --> 00:54:26.640
Well, Warren shared with us that this was one of his favorite comedy bits.

1020
00:54:27.320 --> 00:54:31.960
And he said when Michael goes too far with it, they had even more pitch, Jenna.

1021
00:54:32.140 --> 00:54:34.300
They had even more jokes in the pipeline here.

1022
00:54:34.640 --> 00:54:36.840
He said he and Halstead loved writing this runner.

1023
00:54:37.100 --> 00:54:39.200
Here were some that didn't make it in the show.

1024
00:54:39.360 --> 00:54:39.840
Are you ready?

1025
00:54:40.120 --> 00:54:40.780
I'm ready.

1026
00:54:41.300 --> 00:54:46.120
I can't listen to talk radio when I drive because I get scared that someone else is in the car.

1027
00:54:46.920 --> 00:54:47.400
Okay.

1028
00:54:47.900 --> 00:54:52.280
Then there was another one where Michael locks himself in a dog cage at Kmart.

1029
00:54:52.600 --> 00:54:53.940
No one could get him out.

1030
00:54:54.100 --> 00:54:54.760
He was stuck.

1031
00:54:54.880 --> 00:54:56.420
And they had to call the fire department.

1032
00:54:57.240 --> 00:54:57.440
Okay.

1033
00:54:58.500 --> 00:55:06.340
Then there was a take when Michael goes so far and cuts so deep at himself that he makes himself cry.

1034
00:55:07.120 --> 00:55:10.920
And Warren said Steve's performance was so phenomenal.

1035
00:55:11.020 --> 00:55:12.920
It was his favorite take.

1036
00:55:13.120 --> 00:55:14.340
He fought really hard.

1037
00:55:14.520 --> 00:55:16.420
But too many people disagreed.

1038
00:55:16.520 --> 00:55:17.560
They said it was too depressing.

1039
00:55:17.560 --> 00:55:19.240
And Warren lost the battle.

1040
00:55:19.260 --> 00:55:20.800
So it ends with the,

1041
00:55:21.640 --> 00:55:22.280
Wow.

1042
00:55:24.200 --> 00:55:28.540
This was definitely one of those scenes where we would go to the candy bag.

1043
00:55:28.720 --> 00:55:31.180
They would always have extra stuff for scenes like this.

1044
00:55:31.760 --> 00:55:38.540
And I know we say this a lot, you guys, but Steve is so amazing because he could dial it up

1045
00:55:38.540 --> 00:55:42.320
or dial it down or cry or just, he could do anything.

1046
00:55:42.360 --> 00:55:43.800
He was so impressive.

1047
00:55:44.240 --> 00:55:45.440
I would lose myself.

1048
00:55:45.560 --> 00:55:46.320
I've said this before.

1049
00:55:46.460 --> 00:55:49.380
I'd be like, oh crap, I'm supposed to be reacting as Angela Martin.

1050
00:55:49.380 --> 00:55:52.780
And instead I was just there like, oh my gosh, Steve is crushing it.

1051
00:55:53.120 --> 00:55:54.520
I wish I could have been there.

1052
00:55:55.000 --> 00:55:56.960
I would have loved to have watched this.

1053
00:55:57.100 --> 00:56:02.400
That was maybe one of the things that was so fun about re-watching this episode was that

1054
00:56:02.400 --> 00:56:06.660
I was really watching this as a fan because I wasn't there.

1055
00:56:06.780 --> 00:56:10.720
So I don't have memories of what else went on or what got cut.

1056
00:56:11.000 --> 00:56:12.500
I'm just watching it.

1057
00:56:12.620 --> 00:56:13.200
It's great.

1058
00:56:13.760 --> 00:56:16.740
I think that's why I got so tickled when Andy says,

1059
00:56:16.740 --> 00:56:20.820
it looks like someone's got a case of the definitely's because I wasn't there when you

1060
00:56:20.820 --> 00:56:21.660
guys did that.

1061
00:56:22.080 --> 00:56:22.280
Yeah.

1062
00:56:22.960 --> 00:56:28.440
Well, now, Angela, I've got a fan question for you because you are in the break room

1063
00:56:28.440 --> 00:56:30.280
and you are eating a nut.

1064
00:56:31.860 --> 00:56:32.700
Heather C.

1065
00:56:32.960 --> 00:56:34.840
from Leeds in the UK said,

1066
00:56:35.000 --> 00:56:40.860
Angela, one of my favorite Angela moments ever is the bit where you eat like a squirrel

1067
00:56:40.860 --> 00:56:41.920
in the break room.

1068
00:56:41.920 --> 00:56:46.860
Also, the part where you wrap your packaging back up cracks me up so much.

1069
00:56:47.020 --> 00:56:49.360
How did you come up with this bit exactly?

1070
00:56:49.860 --> 00:56:53.540
Did you perhaps watch a lot of videos of squirrels eating?

1071
00:56:54.720 --> 00:56:56.160
Heather, that's hilarious.

1072
00:56:56.580 --> 00:57:01.060
Well, thank you for appreciating my squirrel eating performance.

1073
00:57:01.660 --> 00:57:04.720
You know, honestly, I didn't watch any videos.

1074
00:57:04.720 --> 00:57:11.620
I just, you know, I've seen a squirrel eat and I use my own personal life observation.

1075
00:57:12.220 --> 00:57:16.760
And just so you know, the stage direction in the script for the scene was,

1076
00:57:17.220 --> 00:57:20.040
Meredith, Kevin, Angela, and Oscar sit at tables.

1077
00:57:20.760 --> 00:57:25.060
Angela gnaws at an almond, holding it with two hands like a squirrel.

1078
00:57:25.660 --> 00:57:29.260
So I knew I had to hold it with two hands, just the way I'm doing it.

1079
00:57:29.640 --> 00:57:32.640
And then I just ate it the way I thought a squirrel would eat it.

1080
00:57:33.120 --> 00:57:38.860
And then in the script, it goes on to say, she puts the rest of her almond back into saran wrap.

1081
00:57:39.600 --> 00:57:40.960
So that was in the script.

1082
00:57:40.960 --> 00:57:44.800
But how I folded it and did it, you know, it was just me sort of playing in the moment.

1083
00:57:45.320 --> 00:57:47.180
I had so much fun doing that.

1084
00:57:47.180 --> 00:57:48.380
And we kept laughing.

1085
00:57:48.780 --> 00:57:52.100
Like Brian and Oscar, we all kept cracking up.

1086
00:57:52.400 --> 00:57:57.840
Well, as your BFF, I will go on record to say that you do not actually eat like that.

1087
00:57:58.320 --> 00:58:00.960
We all know you bite right into the string cheese.

1088
00:58:00.960 --> 00:58:01.720
Oh, yeah.

1089
00:58:01.940 --> 00:58:04.040
I'm not holding it with two hands, gnawing at it.

1090
00:58:04.100 --> 00:58:05.060
I'm going to chomp at it.

1091
00:58:05.120 --> 00:58:07.140
You're not stringing it or anything.

1092
00:58:07.460 --> 00:58:08.840
You're just eating it.

1093
00:58:09.080 --> 00:58:13.080
But you do often wrap up the second half of whatever you haven't eaten.

1094
00:58:13.240 --> 00:58:13.960
This is true.

1095
00:58:14.060 --> 00:58:14.320
That is true.

1096
00:58:14.480 --> 00:58:15.160
This is true.

1097
00:58:15.400 --> 00:58:20.620
If you open my fridge right now, there's like four grapes wrapped up in a little piece of tinfoil.

1098
00:58:20.820 --> 00:58:22.040
You're going to eat those later.

1099
00:58:22.280 --> 00:58:22.880
I will.

1100
00:58:22.880 --> 00:58:32.800
So listen, back in the meeting with Pam and Andy, Andy is really gets a little out of control.

1101
00:58:33.020 --> 00:58:37.080
He's like smothering Pam's belly with his face.

1102
00:58:37.340 --> 00:58:40.020
Well, they've just finally decided to lean into it.

1103
00:58:40.200 --> 00:58:40.900
Yeah, fine.

1104
00:58:40.900 --> 00:58:41.600
We're a couple.

1105
00:58:41.800 --> 00:58:44.260
This is our baby to try to make the sale.

1106
00:58:44.500 --> 00:58:47.120
But we learn a lot about Andy in this moment.

1107
00:58:47.580 --> 00:58:48.180
We do.

1108
00:58:48.500 --> 00:58:55.520
And afterwards, Pam says, you know, that was great, but maybe you took it a little far.

1109
00:58:55.760 --> 00:58:57.780
And he's like, yeah, yeah, it was.

1110
00:58:57.900 --> 00:59:00.240
Maybe the belly kissing was a little much.

1111
00:59:00.700 --> 00:59:03.020
And he reveals a couple of things.

1112
00:59:03.420 --> 00:59:12.840
One, he reveals that it was nice for him to pretend for a minute that he was part of a couple with a baby on the way.

1113
00:59:13.060 --> 00:59:14.440
Oh, that broke my heart.

1114
00:59:14.680 --> 00:59:15.320
I know.

1115
00:59:15.320 --> 00:59:21.440
Oh, and then he also reveals that maybe he has a little crush on Aaron.

1116
00:59:22.120 --> 00:59:22.600
Hmm.

1117
00:59:23.200 --> 00:59:23.560
Hmm.

1118
00:59:24.120 --> 00:59:24.780
Well, guess what?

1119
00:59:24.920 --> 00:59:30.480
Meredith's roommate is friends with the night janitor at the office building where Michael fell.

1120
00:59:30.480 --> 00:59:32.940
And she's got a copy of the security footage.

1121
00:59:33.220 --> 00:59:34.360
Yes, she does.

1122
00:59:34.760 --> 00:59:42.600
Everyone is going to gather around Meredith's computer and we are going to see Michael fall into the koi ponds.

1123
00:59:43.200 --> 00:59:51.360
We got so many fan questions about the fall into the koi pond, and we're going to do our best to break it down for you.

1124
00:59:51.360 --> 01:00:01.360
Starting with Callie B from Rogers, Arkansas, who asked, where was the security footage of Steve falling into the pond filmed?

1125
01:00:02.200 --> 01:00:06.500
Randy told us that we shot the koi pond just down the street from our studios.

1126
01:00:06.920 --> 01:00:11.900
They were a neon sign manufacturing business called Knights of Neon.

1127
01:00:12.300 --> 01:00:18.500
This business has operated for over 30 years, and they make custom neon signs for commercial use.

1128
01:00:18.500 --> 01:00:25.140
I really hope their logo is like a knight in, like, metal armor holding up like a shield.

1129
01:00:25.380 --> 01:00:26.620
Knights of Neon.

1130
01:00:27.020 --> 01:00:31.220
Oh, well, it's spelled N-I-G-H-T-S of Neon.

1131
01:00:31.260 --> 01:00:31.940
Oh, boo.

1132
01:00:32.720 --> 01:00:33.160
Yeah.

1133
01:00:33.540 --> 01:00:34.040
I'm kidding.

1134
01:00:34.220 --> 01:00:34.600
I'm kidding.

1135
01:00:35.840 --> 01:00:42.880
This company, Knights of Neon, had a really big empty warehouse, and that's why we picked it.

1136
01:00:42.880 --> 01:00:52.780
We rented it out for a whole week, and Michael Gallenberg and his construction coordinator, Tim James, they started by laying down, like,

1137
01:00:52.840 --> 01:00:56.620
a waterproof liner over the entire floor of the warehouse.

1138
01:00:57.220 --> 01:01:08.500
Then they built a koi pond on top of it that held 1,000 gallons of filtered water, and then they made the walkways on top of it.

1139
01:01:08.500 --> 01:01:12.180
But they also then set up two cameras.

1140
01:01:12.900 --> 01:01:21.460
Reggie really wanted, like, this motorized surveillance camera that would sort of sweep the lobby back and forth.

1141
01:01:21.760 --> 01:01:29.500
And so Matt Soane created this effect by using a tripod with a fluid head mounted on top of a tall ladder.

1142
01:01:29.500 --> 01:01:33.460
So they made this fakie, sweepie security camera.

1143
01:01:33.460 --> 01:01:39.180
I guess this sweeping shot is best seen in the deleted scenes around four minutes, nine seconds.

1144
01:01:39.540 --> 01:01:40.120
Oh, yeah.

1145
01:01:40.320 --> 01:01:42.000
Go to the deleted scenes for this episode.

1146
01:01:42.000 --> 01:01:43.240
It's the last one.

1147
01:01:43.280 --> 01:01:47.840
It's the whole entire footage of Michael falling in.

1148
01:01:48.180 --> 01:01:51.340
And when I watched it, I was like, oh, wow.

1149
01:01:51.520 --> 01:01:55.100
I didn't realize how long Steve was in the water.

1150
01:01:55.380 --> 01:01:57.600
I mean, they really went for it.

1151
01:01:58.660 --> 01:02:07.360
Well, I guess we added another stationary, non-moving B camera that was angled a little bit more at the front.

1152
01:02:07.900 --> 01:02:11.320
Derek Carver was our B camera operator that day.

1153
01:02:11.480 --> 01:02:19.640
And the footage that you end up seeing on this tape that everyone's watching is that B camera footage, the still footage.

1154
01:02:20.120 --> 01:02:20.820
All right.

1155
01:02:21.120 --> 01:02:22.240
Next question.

1156
01:02:22.520 --> 01:02:24.540
This one comes from Braylon M.

1157
01:02:24.740 --> 01:02:26.000
in Seattle, Washington.

1158
01:02:26.000 --> 01:02:30.760
Were there actually koi in the pond that Michael falls in?

1159
01:02:31.400 --> 01:02:32.160
Yes.

1160
01:02:32.920 --> 01:02:33.560
Yes.

1161
01:02:33.860 --> 01:02:42.060
Randy told us that we went with our usual animal training company, which was Bob Dunn's Animal Rentals in Sylmar, California.

1162
01:02:42.500 --> 01:02:44.420
They do it all, clearly.

1163
01:02:44.800 --> 01:02:45.740
They do.

1164
01:02:46.140 --> 01:02:54.040
The head trainer there is Denise Sanders, but we had to also employ a special koi wrangler named Mike Long.

1165
01:02:54.040 --> 01:02:56.340
What was Kelly Cantley thinking?

1166
01:02:56.720 --> 01:02:59.120
She's had a fruit fly wrangler.

1167
01:02:59.300 --> 01:03:01.220
She's had a koi wrangler.

1168
01:03:01.720 --> 01:03:03.420
Several cat wranglers.

1169
01:03:04.100 --> 01:03:04.800
Fascinating.

1170
01:03:05.240 --> 01:03:13.140
They filled up the koi pond with water several days in advance because they had to let the chlorine evaporate from the water.

1171
01:03:13.640 --> 01:03:16.740
They did a careful pH balance, all of that.

1172
01:03:17.440 --> 01:03:19.780
Randy said the koi were very expensive.

1173
01:03:19.780 --> 01:03:28.000
And before they put the koi into the pond, they did a test with some goldfish just to make sure.

1174
01:03:28.520 --> 01:03:34.740
So the goldfish, they were like, you know, when the king has a tester, eat the food firSt.

1175
01:03:35.040 --> 01:03:35.240
Yeah.

1176
01:03:35.920 --> 01:03:38.880
So those little goldfish, they lived.

1177
01:03:39.240 --> 01:03:41.160
Well, thank God they lived.

1178
01:03:41.480 --> 01:03:41.530
I know.

1179
01:03:41.530 --> 01:03:43.280
The koi went in.

1180
01:03:43.540 --> 01:03:49.060
He said that we used 28 koi that were up to two feet long.

1181
01:03:49.240 --> 01:03:50.560
They're very big.

1182
01:03:50.760 --> 01:03:58.780
And that the total cost for the fish, the holding tanks, the pumps, the filters, and all the labor was $12,000.

1183
01:03:59.760 --> 01:04:02.820
Wow, that's a pricey koi pond.

1184
01:04:03.160 --> 01:04:16.140
Well, when we started filming, Halstead shared that when they put the koi in the pond, they immediately all swam under the steps that went over the middle, you know,

1185
01:04:16.240 --> 01:04:17.080
of the koi pond.

1186
01:04:17.240 --> 01:04:17.780
Yeah.

1187
01:04:17.780 --> 01:04:20.560
And I guess they were in a new environment.

1188
01:04:20.560 --> 01:04:22.280
And so they kind of go and hide.

1189
01:04:22.480 --> 01:04:23.700
They check it out.

1190
01:04:23.920 --> 01:04:28.380
And it takes them a bit to get comfortable and then to swim out from their hiding place.

1191
01:04:28.740 --> 01:04:36.680
The koi wrangler told Halstead and Warren that they had to wait for the koi to settle and come out before they could do the first take.

1192
01:04:37.040 --> 01:04:40.180
Aw, those guys were just standing there waiting for the koi.

1193
01:04:40.340 --> 01:04:40.600
Yeah.

1194
01:04:40.600 --> 01:04:45.380
So someone probably made an announcement, guys, we're going to take five and let the koi get ready.

1195
01:04:46.440 --> 01:04:47.620
Waiting on the koi.

1196
01:04:48.440 --> 01:04:52.140
I've got another fan question now from Nina D in Washington.

1197
01:04:52.700 --> 01:04:56.400
How many takes did you do of Michael falling into the pond?

1198
01:04:57.180 --> 01:05:00.400
Randy said they really hoped the answer would be one.

1199
01:05:00.820 --> 01:05:01.380
Hmm.

1200
01:05:02.000 --> 01:05:03.940
But the answer is two.

1201
01:05:04.440 --> 01:05:05.000
Uh-oh.

1202
01:05:05.460 --> 01:05:08.880
I think we should mention that Steve did not have a stunt double.

1203
01:05:09.200 --> 01:05:11.400
Steve actually fell into the pond.

1204
01:05:11.640 --> 01:05:15.020
That is very clear if you go to deleted scenes.

1205
01:05:16.580 --> 01:05:25.700
When he got out from doing it the first time, they went over and they checked that sweeping camera that they had set up.

1206
01:05:25.800 --> 01:05:27.280
To look like security footage?

1207
01:05:27.280 --> 01:05:29.980
It had missed all of the action.

1208
01:05:30.220 --> 01:05:30.740
Oh!

1209
01:05:31.180 --> 01:05:31.460
Oh!

1210
01:05:31.460 --> 01:05:36.960
It swept past him and then he fell and then it swept back.

1211
01:05:36.960 --> 01:05:38.180
It missed it all.

1212
01:05:38.600 --> 01:05:39.000
Oh.

1213
01:05:39.200 --> 01:05:42.500
So they had to ask Steve to dry off and do it again.

1214
01:05:42.920 --> 01:05:53.580
Warren shared that he could tell Steve was, you know, bummed out because he had fallen into the koi pond and splashed about and now he was going to have to do it again.

1215
01:05:53.580 --> 01:05:59.300
And he said, Steve asked out loud if anyone would believe this was even possible.

1216
01:05:59.920 --> 01:06:07.180
And Paul Lieberstein, Warren's brother, who was there at the time, pointed at Warren and said, oh, it's possible.

1217
01:06:07.600 --> 01:06:08.440
Oh, no.

1218
01:06:08.440 --> 01:06:15.260
